    "q-332303": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332303",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=12x-8.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x-8\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(\\alpha -6\\beta )=12x-8.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -6\\beta =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
